NAME
    horus - overview of the system

DESCRIPTION
    Horus is a capability-based microkernel for x86-64. The kernel is
    written in C, with its security core (capabilities, cryptography,
    parsers) in no_std Rust. Everything else runs in ring 3: init, which
    starts the system; fs_server, the filesystem; console_server, the
    screen and keyboard; and the shell.

    Authority comes from capabilities, never from identity or position
    (capabilities(7)). A login has an identity, which the kernel attests
    and nothing else can claim, but what a program may do is what its
    capabilities say.

    The boot is measured: the firmware measures the boot image, which pins
    the kernel's hash, and the kernel measures its command line, its
    module manifest and each module into the TPM. The volume key is sealed
    to those measurements, so a changed boot cannot unlock the volume.

    The volume is encrypted and authenticated block by block, with a
    rollback tree over it. Swap, where configured, is sealed under a key
    that lives only in memory for one boot.

